@Transactional annotation in springboot ->
 ensures that multiple database operations are executed atomically as a single transaction , providing
 commit on success or rollback on failure

 in simple terms -> if we are doing multiple task or method calls in single method and annoated it with @Transacational
 annoatation in springboot , then all the operations are executed as a single transaction or single unit of work
  if any of the operation fails due to some runtime exception or any network issue or any reason of failure
  then it will be rollback completely nothing will save in Database

@Transacational follows ACID property
A stands for Atomicity -> all the operations are executed as a single transaction or single unit of work
C stands for Consistency -> Enusres that Database state should be conistent befor and after transaction
I stands for Isolation -> it enusures that if multiple transaction are running in parrallel they should
  not interfare with each other
D stands for Durability -> it enusures that commited or compeleted transaction data will nevwe be lost despite of
 system failure

there are two different senrios of transaction in springboot
1 -> runtime exception -> transactional work probperly if there is runtime exception or point failure
   transaction roll back to its initial state
2 -> in checked exception -> transactional not works properly , the point where have putted the checked
